  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday through next Friday)\nIssued at 103 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nSoggy conditions are expected Sunday into Monday with a continued\nsteady rain for most. The rain again mostly remains of light to \nmoderate intensity, but like mentioned within the short term \ndiscussion, we'll see some embedded heavier cells and/or \nthunderstorms. A risk for isolated instances of flooding, persists\nand again would mostly concentrate with any training of heavier \ncells across the urban areas or across locations that are most \nsaturated with earlier rains during the event. WPC highlights a \nlevel 1 to 2 risk for flooding across the vast majority of our \nregion from Sunday into Monday. Overall, we are looking at the 60 \nhour forecast amounts generally ranging from 1 to 3 inches from \nSaturday through Monday night. Isolated higher totals of up to \naround 5 inches will be possible, concentrating best across our \nsouthern tier of counties along the border with the CRP WFO office\nCWA. Conditions remain raw as well with the daytime highs in the \nupper 50s to mid 60s under overcast conditions. \n\nThe storm system exits the region entering midweek but models do \nkeep coastal troughing across South Texas. The region will mostly \ndry out but there could be an isolated PoP a time or two, mainly \nacross the Coastal Plains and our southern tier counties. Cloud \nbreaks should allow for more sunshine and for the temperatures to \nbounce back into the 70s for most locations. A cold front could \narrive sometime late week with help from upper level troughing in \nthe Ohio Valley. We'll monitor how the front could interact with \nlingering coastal troughing in South Texas, but for now the \nofficial forecast into next Friday looks to stay primarily mild \nand dry.",
